{#
    `.. example::` — the source, and under it that source rendered.

    The block is drawn by the template every other code block is drawn by, so
    it is highlighted on the server and carries the copy button: what a reader
    takes away is the body that produced the thing below it.

    `.sds-example` is the frame: dashed and unfilled, which is what says the
    box is not part of the page and leaves what is inside it on the ground it
    would really stand on. Nothing is invented — the class layer draws it.

    The blocks stand in it directly. They carry their own step, and the frame
    takes the last one off — a container between them would state one distance
    for a heading and a paragraph alike, and mostly wrap a single child.
#}
{{ renderNode(node.source) }}
<div class="sds-example{% if node.option('class') %} {{ node.option('class') }}{% endif %}">
    {%- for child in node.children -%}
        {{ renderNode(child) }}
    {%- endfor -%}
</div>
